Keep the CRs of a file read by r with -b.
The -b option keeps the CR of each CRLF line ending in the input, but
the r command read its file with xmalloc_fgetline(), which on Windows
removes a CR before the LF, so with -b the input lines kept their CRLF
endings while the lines inserted by r got bare LFs, and a CRLF file
edited in place with -b came out with mixed line endings. With -b,
read the file with xmalloc_fgets() and remove only the LF.

---
 editors/sed.c |    9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

--- a/editors/sed.c
+++ b/editors/sed.c
@@ -1444,6 +1444,15 @@
 			rfile = fopen_for_read(sed_cmd->string);
 			if (rfile) {
 				char *line;
+#if ENABLE_PLATFORM_MINGW32
+				/* xmalloc_fgetline() removes a CR before the LF */
+				if (G.keep_cr) {
+					while ((line = xmalloc_fgets(rfile)) != NULL) {
+						chomp(line);
+						append(line);
+					}
+				} else
+#endif
 				while ((line = xmalloc_fgetline(rfile))
 						!= NULL)
 					append(line);
